# The PostgreSQL extension lane: kamu-money-pg and its YugabyteDB harness.
#
# THIS IS A SEPARATE WORKSPACE FROM THE REPOSITORY ROOT, and its recipes are separate from the
# root Justfile's for the same reasons the workspace is: this lane patches pgrx to a git fork,
# needs `panic = "unwind"` profiles that cargo honours only at a workspace root, and requires a
# higher MSRV than the nine published crates. Reach it from above with `just pg <recipe>`.
#
# Every routine action has a name here. Not a convenience wrapper: a bare `cargo test` runs a
# DIFFERENT set of tests than this lane's contract requires, because the pgrx suite does not build
# on the host at all. The correct invocation is long, version-specific and easy to get subtly
# wrong -- so it is written down once, here, instead of being re-derived each time by a human or
# an agent.
#
#   just pg doctor          # can this machine run any of it?
#   just pg gate-offline    # format, lint, docs, and the checks that need nothing external
#   just pg gate-pg         # the above plus every container-backed suite (NOT the native YB build)
#   just pg gate-pg-release # gate-pg plus every native YugabyteDB proof -- the release criterion,
#                           # because YugabyteDB is a first-class target. SIX of them, and the
#                           # count is derived from the SUITES array inside the recipe rather than
#                           # kept by hand here: byte-exactness (yb-ab), the whole #[pg_test]
#                           # contract (test-yb-regress), every node of a cluster
#                           # (test-yb-cluster), conservation under concurrency
#                           # (test-yb-concurrent), a read replica (test-yb-readreplica), and a
#                           # dump/restore round trip (test-yb-restore)
#
# THE GATE NAMES CARRY A `gate-` PREFIX BECAUSE THE OLD ONES COLLIDED, and two of them collided
# while meaning OPPOSITE things. In the repository this came from, `check` was the offline gate and
# `check-all` was a heavy one -- four extension builds and a ~1.6 GB image pull. In the root
# Justfile here, `check` is scoped to ONE crate and `check-all` is the FAST inner loop. Anyone
# moving between the two mistypes it exactly once, and the expensive direction is the one that
# looks cheap. So: `check` -> `gate-offline`, `check-all` -> `gate-pg`, `release-check` ->
# `gate-pg-release`, and no name now means two things anywhere in this repository.
#
# SHELL LINTING AND THE SECRET SCAN ARE NOT HERE. The root owns `lint-shell` and `scrub` for the
# whole repository, including every script under this directory -- `lint-shell` checks them with
# its working directory set HERE, because they resolve siblings relative to this lane. Duplicating
# either would give the repository two implementations of one policy, and the forgotten copy is
# always the one that stops matching.
#
# Operational and measurement recipes, deliberately outside every gate -- they restart nodes, or
# produce numbers rather than verdicts:
#
#   just pg yb-resilience # node restart, node failure with writes continuing, rejoin
#   just pg yb-pin-check  # is the YB tag still the digest the pgrx fork was validated against?
#   just pg bench-yb      # kmoney vs text vs numeric on a cluster; a baseline, not a threshold
#   just pg yb-soak       # sustained double-entry traffic, conservation asserted every round
#
# Fleet rules that apply to anything added below: always 127.0.0.1, never `localhost` and
# never 0.0.0.0; every container's lifetime belongs to the thing that started it.

set shell := ["bash", "-uc"]

# The PostgreSQL majors kamu-money-pg is contracted to support. PG15 is the floor.
PG_MAJORS := "15 16 17 18"

# WHERE kamu-money-core COMES FROM, injected on the command line and never written into a manifest.
#
# `kamu-money-pg/Cargo.toml` declares a plain `kamu-money-core = "0.1"`. Until that version is on
# crates.io nothing here resolves without this patch -- a bare `cargo build` in this lane fails with
# `error: no matching package named 'kamu-money-core' found`, which is the intended, loud behaviour.
#
# ON THE COMMAND LINE RATHER THAN IN THE MANIFEST, because a manifest this lane rewrites is a
# manifest this lane can rewrite WRONGLY. The predecessor to the pgrx fork vendored pgrx and
# rewrote it textually at build time, and `re.sub`/`str.replace` return the subject UNCHANGED when
# nothing matches -- so a rewrite that silently did nothing produced a successful build of an
# unshimmed extension. Nothing here edits a manifest, ever. Pass an empty override to resolve the
# published crate instead, which is what a release proof wants:
#
#     just pg lint CORE_PATCH=
#
# THIS DOES NOT REACH INSIDE A CONTAINER, and that is the important limitation. Every Docker build
# here uses the LANE ROOT as its context (`docker build -f kamu-money-pg/Dockerfile ... .`) and the
# Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so `/work` holds this directory and nothing above it. A relative path
# to `../../crates/money-core` does not exist in the image. Container-backed suites therefore need
# kamu-money-core PUBLISHED -- see the note on `test-pg`.
CORE_PATCH := '--config patch.crates-io.kamu-money-core.path="../../crates/money-core"'

# List the recipes. `just` with no argument should teach, not act.
default:
    @just --list --unsorted

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Install what this repository's gates need. Idempotent; safe to re-run.
#
# It installs only what it can do WITHOUT sudo and without inventing a policy: rustup components
# and, if missing or mismatched, cargo-pgrx at the version kamu-money-pg pins. Anything needing a
# system package manager or a PostgreSQL build is NAMED with its exact command rather than
# attempted -- a setup recipe that half-runs `sudo` is worse than one that tells you what to type.
[doc("Install the toolchain pieces the gates need. Idempotent.")]
setup:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    # NOT `set -e`: like `doctor`, this should report everything it cannot do in one run rather
    # than stopping at the first gap. It exits non-zero only if something it OWNS failed.
    set -uo pipefail
    rc=0

    if ! command -v rustup >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "setup: rustup not found -- this machine's rust came from somewhere else."
        echo "setup: install these components however that toolchain does it: rustfmt, clippy, rust-src"
        rc=1
    else
        # NO rust-src HERE, deliberately. It was in this list because the repository this came from
        # held a compile-fail golden that quotes core's own source, and rustc renders that block
        # only when the sources are on disk. That suite is `kamu-money-core`'s and now lives in the
        # main workspace, where the root `just setup` installs the component for it. This lane has
        # no trybuild test at all, so claiming the dependency here would be a requirement nothing
        # in this directory can justify.
        for c in rustfmt clippy; do
            if rustup component add "$c" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                echo "setup: rustup component $c ok"
            else
                echo "setup: FAILED to add rustup component $c" >&2
                rc=1
            fi
        done
    fi

    # cargo-pgrx must match the pinned pgrx EXACTLY -- it refuses to build an extension whose pgrx
    # version differs from its own -- so this reads the pin rather than taking latest.
    want_pgrx=$(grep -m1 '^pgrx = ' kamu-money-pg/Cargo.toml | grep -oE '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+')
    if [ -z "$want_pgrx" ]; then
        echo "setup: could not read the pinned pgrx version from kamu-money-pg/Cargo.toml" >&2
        rc=1
    elif command -v cargo-pgrx >/dev/null 2>&1 && \
         [ "$(cargo pgrx --version | awk '{print $2}')" = "$want_pgrx" ]; then
        echo "setup: cargo-pgrx $want_pgrx already matches the pin"
    else
        echo "setup: installing cargo-pgrx $want_pgrx (this compiles; it takes a few minutes)"
        if cargo install --locked cargo-pgrx --version "$want_pgrx"; then
            echo "setup: cargo-pgrx $want_pgrx ok"
        else
            echo "setup: FAILED to install cargo-pgrx $want_pgrx" >&2
            rc=1
        fi
    fi

    # NEEDS ROOT, so it is named and not attempted. `just shellcheck` hard-fails without it rather
    # than passing vacuously, which is the right behaviour but only helps if you know the fix.
    if command -v shellcheck >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "setup: shellcheck present"
    else
        # Read off /etc/os-release rather than guessing from the kernel: this fleet is CachyOS,
        # which is Arch-derived and has no `apt`.
        id=$(. /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null && echo "${ID:-} ${ID_LIKE:-}")
        case "$id" in
            *arch*)             cmd="sudo pacman -S shellcheck" ;;
            *debian*|*ubuntu*)  cmd="sudo apt-get install shellcheck" ;;
            *fedora*|*rhel*)    cmd="sudo dnf install ShellCheck" ;;
            *)                  cmd="install 'shellcheck' with this system's package manager" ;;
        esac
        echo "setup: shellcheck MISSING -- needs root, so run it yourself: $cmd"
        rc=1
    fi

    echo
    echo "setup: not done here, and deliberately -- 'just pgrx-init' builds a PostgreSQL against a"
    echo "setup: system pg_config, which is a choice (which major?) and several minutes. Run it if"
    echo "setup: you want 'just check-pg' and the kamu-money-pg clippy leg to work locally."
    echo "setup: now run 'just doctor'."
    exit "$rc"

# Check every prerequisite and report what this machine can and cannot run.
doctor:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    # Deliberately NOT `set -e`: a doctor that dies on its first finding tells you one
    # problem per run. It runs every check, then exits non-zero only for a HARD failure.
    set -uo pipefail
    hard=0
    soft=0
    ok()   { printf '  \033[32mok\033[0m    %s\n' "$1"; }
    warn() { printf '  \033[33mwarn\033[0m  %s\n' "$1"; soft=$((soft+1)); }
    fail() { printf '  \033[31mFAIL\033[0m  %s\n' "$1"; hard=$((hard+1)); }

    echo "toolchain"
    if command -v cargo >/dev/null; then
        ok "cargo $(cargo --version | cut -d' ' -f2)"
    else
        fail "cargo not found -- nothing in this repo can build"
    fi
    if command -v rustc >/dev/null; then
        have=$(rustc --version | cut -d' ' -f2)
        want=$(grep -m1 'rust-version' Cargo.toml | tr -d '" ' | cut -d= -f2)
        # Sort-based compare: the MSRV is a floor, and a newer toolchain satisfies it.
        if [ "$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$want" "$have" | sort -V | head -1)" = "$want" ]; then
            ok "rustc $have (workspace rust-version = $want)"
        else
            fail "rustc $have is below the workspace rust-version $want"
        fi
    else
        fail "rustc not found"
    fi
    cargo fmt --version   >/dev/null 2>&1 && ok "rustfmt"  || fail "rustfmt missing (rustup component add rustfmt)"
    cargo clippy --version >/dev/null 2>&1 && ok "clippy"   || fail "clippy missing (rustup component add clippy)"
    # rust-src IS NOT CHECKED HERE ANY MORE. It guarded a compile-fail golden that quotes core's
    # own source, and that suite belongs to `kamu-money-core` in the main workspace -- the root
    # `just doctor` checks it there, against the gate that actually needs it. Nothing under this
    # directory uses trybuild, so a check here would report on a component this lane never loads
    # and would pass or fail for reasons no recipe below can act on.

    echo "containers"
    if command -v docker >/dev/null; then
        if docker info >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            ok "docker daemon reachable"
            # This host shares ONE daemon across several orgs' runners. Never global-prune.
            n=$(docker ps -q | wc -l)
            ok "$n container(s) running on the shared daemon -- never global-prune here"
            # `-a` MINUS the running ones. `docker ps -aq` counts live containers too, so
            # running `just doctor` while `just test-pg` is in flight reported the matrix's own
            # working containers as leaks -- and advised `just containers`, which would have
            # killed the run it was describing. A diagnostic that tells you to break something
            # is worse than no diagnostic. Measured: two majors build concurrently, so the
            # false warning appeared on every concurrent invocation, not as a rare race.
            all=$(docker ps -aq --filter 'label=kamu-money-pg.revision' | wc -l)
            live=$(docker ps -q --filter 'label=kamu-money-pg.revision' | wc -l)
            leaked=$((all - live))
            if [ "$leaked" -gt 0 ]; then
                warn "$leaked leaked test-matrix container(s); run: just containers"
            elif [ "$live" -gt 0 ]; then
                ok "no leaked test-matrix containers ($live still running -- a matrix is live)"
            else
                ok "no leaked test-matrix containers"
            fi
        else
            warn "docker installed but the daemon is unreachable -- container suites will be skipped"
        fi
    else
        warn "docker not found -- test-db, test-yb and test-pg cannot run"
    fi

    echo "kamu-money-pg (pgrx)"
    # PGRX_HOME is pinned to .pgrx/ by .cargo/config.toml, so this is a property of the
    # CHECKOUT rather than of whoever is sitting at the machine.
    if [ -f ".pgrx/config.toml" ]; then
        ok "in-repo .pgrx present -- 'just check-pg' compiles kamu-money-pg without a container"
    else
        # Not a hard failure: containers remain the supported path for actually RUNNING the
        # suite. But without this, every kamu-money-pg mistake costs a two-minute container round
        # trip instead of a two-second cargo check.
        warn "no .pgrx/ -- run 'just pgrx-init'; until then kamu-money-pg can only be built in containers"
    fi
    if command -v cargo-pgrx >/dev/null; then
        have_pgrx=$(cargo pgrx --version | awk '{print $2}')
        want_pgrx=$(grep -m1 '^pgrx = ' kamu-money-pg/Cargo.toml | grep -oE '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+')
        if [ "$have_pgrx" = "$want_pgrx" ]; then
            ok "cargo-pgrx $have_pgrx matches the pinned pgrx dependency"
        else
            # cargo-pgrx refuses to build an extension whose pgrx version differs from its own.
            fail "cargo-pgrx $have_pgrx but kamu-money-pg pins pgrx $want_pgrx -- they must match"
        fi
    else
        warn "cargo-pgrx not installed -- 'just pgrx-init' and 'just test-pg' need it"
    fi

    echo "hygiene"
    if git rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        ok "inside a git work tree"
        # THE CRATE MUST INHERIT ITS IDENTITY, NOT RESTATE IT. This block used to reconcile
        # `repository` in kamu-money-core and kamu-money-iso against the git remote, because the
        # repository this came from had no remote at all and those manifests named one anyway.
        # Both crates are elsewhere now and this repository has a remote, so that reconciliation
        # has nothing left to compare.
        #
        # What replaces it guards the thing the transplant actually got wrong: kamu-money-pg
        # arrived carrying `license = "MIT"` and a `repository` pointing at the experimental
        # repository it came from. Both are inherited from the lane's workspace root now, which is
        # also where `publish = false` lives -- and a hard-coded value here would silently win over
        # the root and put a stale URL, or a publishable flag, on a crate that must never publish.
        if grep -qE '^(repository|license|publish) = ' kamu-money-pg/Cargo.toml; then
            warn "kamu-money-pg/Cargo.toml hard-codes repository/license/publish -- these belong to [workspace.package] in the lane root; a literal here overrides it"
        else
            ok "kamu-money-pg inherits repository, license and publish from the lane root"
        fi
        declared="$(grep -m1 '^repository = ' Cargo.toml 2>/dev/null | cut -d'"' -f2 || true)"
        actual="$(git remote get-url "$(git remote | head -1)" 2>/dev/null || echo '')"
        if [ -z "$declared" ]; then
            warn "the lane root declares no 'repository'"
        else
            case "$actual" in
                *"${declared#https://github.com/}"*) ok "lane root 'repository' matches the git remote" ;;
                *) warn "lane root says '$declared' but the remote is '$actual'" ;;
            esac
        fi
    else
        fail "not a git work tree"
    fi

    # The .gitignore path for the interception certificates is LOAD-BEARING, and it silently
    # went stale the moment the crate directory was renamed -- a real CA certificate, whose
    # subject embeds a tenant identifier, surfaced as untracked and one `git add -A` from
    # being published. A rule that only holds while a path stays put is not a rule, so this
    # asks git directly rather than trusting the glob to still match.
    leaked_certs=$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all 2>/dev/null | grep -cE '\.(crt|pem|key)$')
    if [ "$leaked_certs" -gt 0 ]; then
        fail "$leaked_certs certificate/key file(s) are UNTRACKED and not ignored -- fix .gitignore before any commit:"
        git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all | grep -E '\.(crt|pem|key)$' | sed 's/^/          /'
    else
        ok "no untracked certificate or key files"
    fi

    echo
    if [ "$hard" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "doctor: $hard hard failure(s), $soft warning(s)"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "doctor: healthy ($soft warning(s))"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build, format, lint
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Format the workspace.
fmt:
    cargo fmt --all

# Never pipe this into `head` -- doing so swallows the exit code and turns a real failure
# into a silent pass.
#
# `[doc]` rather than a bare comment throughout: `just --list` uses the LAST comment line as
# a recipe's description, so any recipe whose comment explains something renders in the
# listing as a mid-sentence fragment. The default recipe is meant to teach.
[doc("Verify formatting without changing anything.")]
fmt-check:
    cargo fmt --all --check


# PINNED TO ONE MAJOR, NEVER `--all-features`. A pgrx crate's PG-major features are MUTUALLY
# EXCLUSIVE: `--all-features` turns on pg15..pg18 at once and fails to compile, so a workspace-wide
# `--all-features` sweep does not lint this crate strictly -- it fails to build it, and whoever
# wired the sweep excludes it and moves on. That is how the extension went unlinted entirely in the
# repository this came from.
#
# This was `cargo clippy --workspace --exclude kamu-money-pg` followed by the pinned run. There the
# workspace held three crates and the exclusion had something left to lint. Here the workspace IS
# kamu-money-pg, so that first line linted NOTHING while reading exactly like coverage. The other
# two crates are `kamu-money-core` in the main workspace, and `just clippy-money` up there owns them.
[doc("Clippy the extension at the pedantry its crate root denies, pinned to one PG major.")]
lint:
    cargo {{ CORE_PATCH }} clippy -p kamu-money-pg --no-default-features --features pg18 --all-targets -- -D warnings

# Populate the in-repo PGRX_HOME so kamu-money-pg can be compile-checked without a container.
#
# `.cargo/config.toml` already points PGRX_HOME at `.pgrx/` for every cargo invocation, on
# every machine; this fills it in. Needs a host PostgreSQL whose pg_config matches a supported
# major -- it does NOT compile its own, which is the slow path pgrx takes when given no
# pg_config.
[doc("Populate the in-repo PGRX_HOME from the host pg_config.")]
pgrx-init pg_config="/usr/bin/pg_config":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    major=$("{{ pg_config }}" --version | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1)
    echo "initialising .pgrx for PG${major} from {{ pg_config }}"
    cargo pgrx init "--pg${major}" "{{ pg_config }}"
    # THE SYMLINK IS NOT OPTIONAL, and both YB Dockerfiles already carry this same workaround with
    # the same comment -- which is why it belongs here instead: `cargo pgrx init` does NOT honour
    # the `[env]` PGRX_HOME from .cargo/config.toml and writes to the default ~/.pgrx, while
    # pgrx-pg-sys's BUILD SCRIPT (run under cargo) DOES honour it and then fails with
    # "$PGRX_HOME does not exist". Reconciling the two is what makes `just lint` able to reach
    # kamu-money-pg at all; without it the recipe reports success and the next command fails.
    if [ ! -e .pgrx ]; then
        ln -sfn "${PGRX_HOME:-$HOME/.pgrx}" .pgrx
        echo "linked .pgrx -> ${PGRX_HOME:-$HOME/.pgrx} (cargo pgrx init ignores the forced [env] path)"
    fi
    [ -f .pgrx/config.toml ] || { echo "pgrx-init: .pgrx/config.toml still missing" >&2; exit 1; }
    echo "pgrx-init: ready -- 'just check-pg' and the kamu-money-pg clippy leg can now run"

# Type-check kamu-money-pg without a container. Catches the mechanical errors -- wrong module path,
# macro used above its definition -- that otherwise cost a two-minute container round trip.
#
# TWO THINGS IT CANNOT DO, both learned the hard way:
#   1. It proves the crate COMPILES, not that the extension installs or that a test passes.
#      SQL ordering, opclass definitions and transaction semantics are invisible here.
#   2. It only covers the major whose pg_config is in `.pgrx` -- one, on a normal machine.
#      `pq_copymsgbytes` takes `*mut c_void` on PG18 and `*mut c_char` on PG15, so a turbofish
#      cast passed this recipe and failed the matrix. Cross-major differences need `test-pg`.
[doc("Compile-check kamu-money-pg locally against one major (no container, no tests).")]
check-pg pg="18":
    cargo {{ CORE_PATCH }} check -p kamu-money-pg --no-default-features --features pg{{ pg }} --all-targets

# The normalized fingerprint of the SQL surface `kamu-money-pg` generates. THE ORACLE FOR A
# REFACTOR of that crate: pass the hash from before the change as `expect` and the recipe fails
# if the surface moved.
#
# It exists because `sha256sum` over the generated file DOES NOT WORK. pgrx's output is not
# reproducible -- two runs over identical source emit the entities in a different order (E21) --
# so a byte comparison reports a false positive every time and teaches whoever hit it to stop
# believing the check. The script strips the provenance comments that encode source position and
# hashes the sorted object set instead.
#
# `features` reaches the in-backend test surface, which the default build cannot see at all:
# pass `pg_test` when the thing being refactored is the test suite. "64 tests still pass" proves
# the ones that RAN are green, not that the same 64 still exist.
#
# `expect` and `features` are quoted at the call site because they default to empty and `just`
# args are positional.
schema-hash pg="18" expect="" features="":
    ./kamu-money-pg/schema-hash.sh {{ pg }} "{{ expect }}" "{{ features }}"

# Facts about the tree that no compiler checks: the Justfile's gates compose what they claim, the
# scripts merge no docker streams on the host, the control file names the type it ships, the
# licence texts have not drifted.
#
# THESE READ FILES AND LINK NOTHING, which is why they live in their own crate rather than under
# kamu-money-pg/tests/. An integration test there drags in the pgrx build -- a trivial empty one
# fails with `Error: $PGRX_HOME does not exist` -- and that would put PostgreSQL-from-source on the
# critical path of a check that greps a Justfile. See hygiene/Cargo.toml.
#
# nextest, then the doctests, per this repository's cadence: nextest does NOT run doc examples, so
# an unpaired invocation stops testing them silently. The lib here is empty and has none today;
# the pairing is what keeps that true if one is ever added.
[doc("Repository-hygiene guards: sixteen facts about the tree that no compiler checks.")]
test-hygiene:
    cargo {{ CORE_PATCH }} nextest run -p kamu-money-pg-hygiene
    cargo {{ CORE_PATCH }} test -p kamu-money-pg-hygiene --doc

# ONE MAJOR, NOT `--all-features`. The `pgN` features are mutually exclusive -- each selects a
# different pgrx backend -- so `--all-features` does not compile here and never did. `doc-pg-all`
# below is the whole matrix, for when the majors are what changed.
#
# THIS IS IN `gate-offline`, not beside it. It was a standalone recipe in the repository this came
# from, and the result was a green gate sitting next to a red docs build: two rustdoc link errors
# that would have broken the docs.rs page of a crate the gate had just called releasable. A check
# nobody runs is a check that does not exist.
#
# It builds the EXTENSION only. The kernel and the ISO register are `kamu-money-core` in the main
# workspace now, and `just doc-money` up there owns them.
[doc("Build the extension's API docs under one major, failing on a broken intra-doc link.")]
doc-pg:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    export RUSTDOCFLAGS="-D warnings"
    cargo {{ CORE_PATCH }} doc -p kamu-money-pg --no-default-features --features pg18 --no-deps

# The documentation build across every supported PostgreSQL major. Not in `gate-offline`: it is four
# rustdoc runs of the same crate and only the feature-gated items differ between them.
[doc("Build the extension's docs on every supported PostgreSQL major, failing on warnings.")]
doc-pg-all:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    export RUSTDOCFLAGS="-D warnings"
    for pg in 15 16 17 18; do
        echo "=== rustdoc kamu-money-pg pg${pg} ==="
        cargo {{ CORE_PATCH }} doc -p kamu-money-pg --no-default-features --features "pg${pg}" --no-deps
    done

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
#
# Split by what each needs, not by crate: a suite that needs Docker is a different kind of
# thing from one that needs only a compiler, and collapsing them means the cheap ones stop
# being runnable on a machine that cannot host the expensive ones.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# YugabyteDB. Separate because it pulls a ~1.6GB image and takes about a minute.
#
# THE IDENTITY IS RESOLVED HERE, not hard-coded in the test. Until 2026-07-26 the test named
# `yugabytedb/yugabyte:2025.2.4.1-b4` in a Rust constant -- a mutable tag, one release behind the
# digest the native path is pinned to. `gate-pg-release` runs this through `gate-pg`, so the run
# exercised TWO YugabyteDB identities while reading as one, and a retag could change what was
# tested with nothing in the tree moving.
#
# `${KMONEY_YB_IMAGE:-...}` so a caller that has ALREADY resolved an identity -- gate-pg-release
# does, once, for the whole run -- gets that one rather than a second resolution of the same tag.
# Resolving twice is the straddle `yb-image.sh` exists to prevent.
#
# THE SUITE LIVES IN THE MAIN WORKSPACE, THE IMAGE IDENTITY LIVES HERE, and this recipe is the
# seam. `yugabyte_roundtrip` is a kamu-money-core test -- it exercises the TEXT adapter, not the
# extension -- so it belongs to the published crate and stays at crates/money-core. Resolving the
# image is this lane's job, because `yb-image.sh` and the digest pin it enforces are here. Running
# it from up there instead would either duplicate the resolver or hard-code a mutable tag again,
# which is the exact failure the paragraph above records.
[doc("The text adapter against a live YugabyteDB. The suite is in the main workspace; the image identity is resolved here.")]
test-yb tag="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    IMAGE="${KMONEY_YB_IMAGE:-$(./kamu-money-pg/yb/yb-image.sh {{ tag }})}"
    echo "test-yb: text adapter against $IMAGE"
    KMONEY_YB_IMAGE="$IMAGE" \
        cargo test --manifest-path ../../Cargo.toml \
            -p kamu-money-core --features postgres --test yugabyte_roundtrip -- --ignored --nocapture

# The pgrx extension suite, once per supported PostgreSQL major, each in its own container.
# Pass majors to narrow it: `just test-pg "17 18"`.
#
# YugabyteDB is NOT a row in this PGDG matrix -- it is a different AXIS. The *naive* build
# against the YB image fails (E15: the shipped `elog.h` includes `yb/yql/pggate/util/ybc_util.h`,
# which the image does not distribute; and a `.so` built elsewhere fails on its older glibc).
# But kamu-money-pg DOES run natively on YB against the 3-symbol pgrx fork, built
# FROM the YB image with `--features pg15,yb-pg15` -- proven byte-exact by `just yb-native` / `just yb-ab`
# (E16). That gate compiles from the YB image, so it lives BESIDE this matrix, not inside it.
#
# THIS NEEDS kamu-money-core ON crates.io, and CORE_PATCH cannot help. Every Docker build here uses
# the LANE ROOT as its context and the Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so `/work` inside the image
# holds this directory and nothing above it -- `../../crates/money-core` is not in the image, and a
# path patch naming it resolves to nothing. The host-side recipes (`check-pg`, `doc-pg`, `lint`)
# take the patch and work today; every container-backed suite, and therefore `gate-pg` and
# `gate-pg-release`, waits for the published crate. Broadening the build context to the repository
# root would also work and is deliberately not done: it would put all nine published crates into
# every extension image build to satisfy a dependency that is about to exist anyway.
[doc("The pgrx extension suite, once per supported PostgreSQL major, each in a container.")]
test-pg majors=PG_MAJORS jobs="1":
    MATRIX_JOBS={{ jobs }} ./kamu-money-pg/test-matrix.sh {{ majors }}

# The evidence behind kamu-money-core's DESIGN.md E15, re-run against a live YugabyteDB.
#
# Deliberately OUTSIDE `gate-pg`. Every other recipe here asserts that this project works;
# this one asserts that something else does NOT, which is a claim about a third party's
# software on a given day rather than about our tree. A YugabyteDB release that fixes either
# blocker would turn `gate-pg` red on a green working copy -- accurate, but it would train
# whoever hit it to route around the gate. Kept as a named recipe so the evidence is
# reproducible on demand, and so E15 cites a command rather than a memory.
#
# Needs `just test-pg 18` first: it reuses that image as the builder (modern glibc, has cc).
#
# NOTE: this proves the *naive* (unshimmed) build still fails -- the E15 measurement. It does
# NOT contradict `just yb-native`, which builds the SAME extension against the 3-symbol pgrx fork
# and runs it byte-exact on YB (E16). Same code, two builds: naive fails, adapted works.
[doc("Re-run the YugabyteDB incompatibility (NAIVE build) evidence behind kamu-money-core's DESIGN.md E15.")]
yb-probe yb-image="" builder-image="":
    ./kamu-money-pg/yb-compat-probe.sh "{{ yb-image }}" "{{ builder-image }}"

# --- First-class YugabyteDB native gate (E16) -------------------------------------------
# kamu-money-pg runs NATIVELY + byte-exact on YB against a 3-symbol pgrx-0.19.1 fork. These build
# FROM the YB image, so they are heavy (a from-source compile) and kept OUT of gate-pg,
# beside `test-pg`. `yb-ab` is the completion gate: the SAME battery on YB and on stock PG15
# must be byte-identical -- with no ordering/opclass surface, there are zero YB edges.

# THE DEPLOYABLE ARTIFACT, and the one the cluster suites actually run.
#
# `yb-build` below produces loose files, which is what a test harness needs to `docker cp` and what
# the missing-library negative control needs to remove. It is NOT how the extension reaches
# production: installing onto running nodes cannot survive a node being replaced, and requires
# enumerating every tserver -- including read replicas, which are tservers too.
#
# Baking it into the image makes "is the extension on every node?" the same question as "is every
# node on digest D?", which the orchestrator already answers. The digest printed here is the one to
# pin in a deployment, exactly as YB-PINNED.txt pins the base image it derives from.
[doc("Build the DEPLOYABLE node image: the YugabyteDB image with kmoney baked in.")]
yb-node-image tag="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    YB_REF="$(./kamu-money-pg/yb/yb-image.sh {{ tag }})"
    IMAGE_ID="$(./kamu-money-pg/yb/node-image.sh "$YB_REF")"
    echo
    echo "node image built"
    echo "  base      $YB_REF"
    echo "  image id  $IMAGE_ID"
    echo
    echo "Every node -- primary and read replica, RF3 and RF5 -- runs this one image; nothing is"
    echo "ever installed onto a running node."
    echo
    echo "AN IMAGE ID IS NOT A REGISTRY DIGEST. An orchestrator pulls repo@sha256:..., which does"
    echo "not exist until this image is PUSHED, and publishing belongs to the platform release"
    echo "path rather than to this repository. Record the registry digest against the ID above"
    echo "when you publish it; 'just gate-pg-release' certifies the bytes, not their distribution."

[doc("Build the kamu-money-pg artifact against YugabyteDB's PG15 fork (pgrx fork + yb-pg15).")]
yb-build tag="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    # BEFORE THE BUILD, because `-o kamu-money-pg/yb/out` overwrites the artefact triplet a
    # release check may be in the middle of hashing. This recipe took NO lock at all until a
    # 2026-07-26 review pointed out that it is the one entry point nothing could refuse.
    source ./kamu-money-pg/yb/workspace-lock.sh
    workspace_lock "yb-build" || exit 1
    YB_REF="$(./kamu-money-pg/yb/yb-image.sh {{ tag }})"
    echo "yb: image identity $YB_REF"
    docker build -f kamu-money-pg/yb/Dockerfile --target artifact \
        --build-arg YB_IMAGE="$YB_REF" -o kamu-money-pg/yb/out .

# NOT `yb-native: yb-build`. Each would resolve the tag separately, which is the very straddle
# this is meant to prevent -- the identity is resolved ONCE here and passed to both the build
# and the runtime.
[doc("Build for YB, run the ABI battery on a fresh YugabyteDB, print the result.")]
yb-native tag="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    # BEFORE the artifact build below, not inside run-yb.sh at the end of it. `run-yb.sh` takes the
    # lock too, but by then this recipe has already overwritten the shared triplet -- the refusal
    # arrived after the damage, which is the P0-2 shape.
    source ./kamu-money-pg/yb/workspace-lock.sh
    workspace_lock "yb-native" || exit 1
    YB_REF="$(./kamu-money-pg/yb/yb-image.sh {{ tag }})"
    echo "yb: image identity $YB_REF"
    docker build -f kamu-money-pg/yb/Dockerfile --target artifact \
        --build-arg YB_IMAGE="$YB_REF" -o kamu-money-pg/yb/out .
    ./kamu-money-pg/yb/run-yb.sh "$YB_REF" "" kamu-money-pg/yb/out/out-yb.txt kamu-money-pg/yb/abi_battery.sql

# One resolved identity feeds ALL THREE consumers: the YB artifact build, the live YB runtime,
# and the stock-PG15 reference build. Byte-exactness is a claim about one image; resolving the
# tag per-consumer could compare artifacts from two different builds of "the same" version.
# The negative control on its own, against the last battery output. `yb-ab` runs this too; it is
# exposed separately so the oracle can be exercised without a 20-minute rebuild.
[doc("Negative control: prove every battery assertion still rejects a mutated output.")]
yb-selftest out="kamu-money-pg/yb/out/out-yb.txt":
    ./kamu-money-pg/yb/assert-battery-selftest.sh {{ out }}

# --- The ported case suite (P0.1) --------------------------------------------------------------
# The 64 `#[pg_test]`s ARE this type's contract -- 63 of them portable, plus one that asserts a
# stock-PostgreSQL PLAN and says so as NOT-PORTABLE -- and until now they had only ever run on PGDG
# PostgreSQL: `cargo pgrx test` manages its own server and cannot be aimed at YugabyteDB, so the
# entire YB evidence surface was one ~112-line script. `tests/pg_regress` restates that contract as
# sql/ + expected/ pairs which run against any live server, so the same cases and the SAME
# hand-authored goldens are checked on YugabyteDB and on the stock-PG15 reference.
#
# NAMED `test-yb-regress`, NOT the readiness document's `test-yb-native`: `yb-native` already means
# the single-script battery, and two recipes one word apart meaning different suites is exactly the
# kind of name that gets run by mistake at 2am.

[doc("The ported #[pg_test] suite against a live single-node YugabyteDB.")]
test-yb-regress tag="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    ./kamu-money-pg/yb/run-yb-regress.sh "$(./kamu-money-pg/yb/yb-image.sh {{ tag }})"

# The oracle's own negative control: mutate a correct output fourteen ways and require each to be
# rejected FOR ITS OWN REASON. Needs no database, so it sits in `gate-offline` beside the offline tests --
# a suite runner that has rotted into always-passing certifies whatever the code currently does.
[doc("Negative control: prove the case-suite runner still rejects a wrong output.")]
test-regress-selftest:
    ./kamu-money-pg/tests/pg_regress/selftest.sh

# --- Cluster, concurrency, resilience (P0.2, P0.3, P1.2) ---------------------------------------
# Production YugabyteDB is several nodes, and the failures that only exist there are the ones a
# money type cannot be wrong about: a node missing the .so, DDL that must reach every node, a
# tablet split moving rows while values sit in columns, two transactions meeting on one row.

[doc("kmoney on a 3-node RF=3 cluster: every node, a tablet split, and a missing-library control.")]
test-yb-cluster tag="":
